= Using the Playgroup website

The Playgroup website is a practical resource for planning culturally relevant sessions for children aged 0–4, their carers and communities across the Torres Strait. It is organised in the same order as this printed guide: learn about playgroups, prepare through the facilitator toolkit, select an activity, plan the session, then record reflection and reporting information.

The website keeps saved session plans in the browser on the device being used. It does not send the saved plan to the website. When a facilitator chooses *Download PDF*, the completed plan is created and saved directly to that outreach computer. Keep the downloaded file and any printed copy in line with Mura Kosker Sorority Inc. procedures.

== Session rhythm

*Start — approximately 15 minutes.* Welcome children and carers, allow arrivals and settling in, offer a greeting, song or free-play invitation, and create a calm beginning.

*Middle — approximately 20 minutes.* Support free play, shared conversation, a parent discussion or education time, and responsive interaction with children.

*End — approximately 15 minutes.* Offer one structured experience, a goodbye song or ritual, shared pack-up, and a simple preview of the next session.

== Planning and reflection

For each session, enter a session name, community name and session time. Add activities to the Start, Middle or End stage. After the session, record the number of children and carers attending, children’s engagement, emerging learning or interests, and one facilitator reflection or next step. Download the plan when the record is complete.

== Cultural care

Use stories, language, songs, images and activities only where they are appropriate and approved for the local Mura context. Mura Kosker Sorority Inc. should approve any new photograph, cultural knowledge, instructional card, video link or public-facing content before it is added to the website or printed material.

#pagebreak()

= Digital Play

*Description.* Encourages foundational skills in literacy, numeracy, and digital literacy through play-based learning. Offers digital content that reflects Indigenous languages, stories, and traditional knowledge.

*Materials.* Mat for bots; instructional videos and cards; mat and cushions for sitting.

*Before the activity.* Prepare the mat, bots and approved cards before families arrive. Check that digital materials and content are appropriate for the local Mura context. Arrange cushions so children can sit comfortably with a carer nearby.

*During the activity.* Seat children on the mat and cushions. Introduce one digital prompt or story at a time. Encourage children to count, name, move and talk as they play. Allow carers to join children and support turn taking.

*After the activity.* Close with a shared reflection or retelling. Pack away devices, bots and cards safely. Record children’s engagement, the skills observed and a next-step idea in the session plan.

= Activity library

Each activity includes a short description, materials list and a before–during–after facilitator guide. Adapt the activity to children’s interests, session timing and local approval requirements.

== 1. Head, shoulders and toes in local language

*Description.* A familiar movement song adapted with approved local-language words for body parts.

*Materials.* Approved local-language word list; open space.

*Before.* Confirm the approved local-language words and create space for a safe circle. Invite carers to sit or stand with children.

*During.* Gather children in a circle. Introduce the approved local-language words. Model each movement slowly. Sing together and repeat at a comfortable pace.

*After.* Let children choose a favourite movement to repeat. Close the circle calmly and note any words, movements or participation children enjoyed.

== 2. Weaving together

*Description.* A fine-motor activity using age-appropriate locally approved weaving materials.

*Materials.* Approved weaving materials; pre-cut strips; safety scissors for adults.

*Before.* Confirm that the weaving example and materials are approved. Pre-cut strips, check for loose or sharp parts, and give carers a clear place to sit alongside children.

*During.* Show an approved example. Demonstrate a simple over-under movement. Offer pre-cut materials. Support children to take turns and help one another.

*After.* Give children time to show their work. Collect materials safely and record any emerging fine-motor, sharing or cultural-connection observations.

== 3. Local storytelling circle

*Description.* Share an approved story with expressive voice, visual prompts and a comfortable group space.

*Materials.* Approved story or visual prompts; cushions for sitting.

*Before.* Confirm that the story and visual prompts are approved for sharing. Set out cushions in a quiet circle and invite carers to join.

*During.* Create a comfortable circle. Use an approved local story. Pause for simple questions. Invite children to respond through words, gesture or drawing.

*After.* Allow a short retelling or drawing response. Pack away story materials respectfully and record interests or questions that may inform another activity.

== 4. Dance and movement

*Description.* A simple, approved movement activity that supports rhythm, coordination and shared enjoyment.

*Materials.* Open space; approved music or song.

*Before.* Check that the space is safe and the song or music is approved. Explain where children and carers can move safely.

*During.* Demonstrate simple approved movements. Begin slowly. Encourage children to move together at their own pace.

*After.* Offer a calm final movement or breath. Check the space, pack away equipment and note children’s participation and preferred movements.

== 5. Welcome song circle

*Description.* A predictable start that helps children and carers settle into the session together.

*Materials.* Open floor space.

*Before.* Clear enough floor space for a circle and choose a simple greeting song.

*During.* Sit in a circle. Offer a simple greeting song. Welcome each child by name when appropriate. Invite waves, claps or gentle movements.

*After.* Invite a short transition to free play. Note who settled quickly, who needed support and any family follow-up required.

== 6. Finger painting

*Description.* A sensory art activity that invites creative expression and conversation.

*Materials.* Non-toxic paints; large paper; smocks; water for cleaning.

*Before.* Protect the work surface. Set out small amounts of non-toxic paint, large paper, smocks and water for cleaning.

*During.* Offer paper and a small amount of paint. Model a few marks. Allow open exploration. Display work with the child’s consent.

*After.* Support hand washing and safe clean-up. Ask children if they would like their work displayed or taken home. Record conversation and creative interests.

== 7. Building blocks play

*Description.* Shared block building develops problem-solving, spatial awareness and turn taking.

*Materials.* Large soft blocks; play mat.

*Before.* Set blocks on a mat, remove damaged pieces and make enough room for children to build safely.

*During.* Model stacking and balancing. Encourage children to build together. Celebrate creations before pack-up.

*After.* Invite children to photograph or describe their creation if appropriate. Pack blocks away together and record examples of problem-solving or sharing.

== 8. Singing and action songs

*Description.* Music and movement combine to support language, coordination and participation.

*Materials.* Approved song list; optional instruments.

*Before.* Choose age-appropriate approved songs and check any instruments for safety.

*During.* Model actions clearly. Start slowly. Repeat favourites when children show interest.

*After.* Let children suggest a final action. Pack instruments away and note words, movements or songs that supported participation.

== 9. Playdough creations

*Description.* Hands-on exploration that strengthens hand muscles and supports imaginative play.

*Materials.* Playdough; rolling pins; cookie cutters; play mat.

*Before.* Place materials on a clean mat and check that tools are age-appropriate.

*During.* Give each child a small portion. Show rolling and squashing. Offer tools for exploration. Invite children to describe their ideas.

*After.* Store materials safely and clean surfaces. Record children’s imaginative ideas or any fine-motor support needed next time.

== 10. Nature exploration walk

*Description.* A guided outdoor walk for noticing, collecting and talking about safe natural objects.

*Materials.* Small buckets or bags; safe outdoor area.

*Before.* Check the route first, confirm local suitability and explain boundaries to carers and children.

*During.* Stay together. Notice safe objects and sounds. Collect only where appropriate. Return to sort and talk about discoveries.

*After.* Return borrowed natural objects where appropriate, wash hands, and record children’s observations and language.

== 11. Sand and water play

*Description.* Sensory exploration with simple tools encourages curiosity and cooperative play.

*Materials.* Sand tray; water; cups and scoops; towels.

*Before.* Set up a safely supervised area with towels ready. Check sand and water containers are clean and easy to access.

*During.* Provide a small set of tools. Encourage exploration. Model sharing. Supervise water play closely.

*After.* Dry hands and feet, empty water safely, clean the area and note sensory interests or cooperative play.

== 12. Outdoor free play

*Description.* Child-led outdoor play that supports confidence, imagination and physical development.

*Materials.* Safe outdoor space; balls; bubbles (optional).

*Before.* Check the outdoor area, weather, shade and equipment. Invite carers to supervise and join children.

*During.* Invite children to explore. Observe and support safe interactions. Follow children’s interests. Allow time for pack-up.

*After.* Check all children are together, collect equipment and record children’s interests or confidence-building moments.

== 13. Picture book corner

*Description.* A calm reading space for individual exploration alongside peers.

*Materials.* Picture books; cushions; quiet corner.

*Before.* Set up a comfortable quiet area with a small, varied book basket.

*During.* Allow children to choose. Read nearby when invited. Respect quiet exploration.

*After.* Return books to the basket, straighten cushions and note book choices, early-literacy interests or moments of calm.

== 14. Puzzle play

*Description.* Independent puzzle solving near peers builds persistence and fine-motor coordination.

*Materials.* Age-appropriate puzzles; play mat.

*Before.* Set out a few puzzle choices on a mat and check all pieces are safe and complete.

*During.* Invite children to choose. Offer help only when needed. Celebrate persistence and completion.

*After.* Pack puzzles carefully, note emerging problem-solving skills and consider which puzzles to offer next time.

== 15. Colouring station

*Description.* A shared-material activity that supports fine-motor development and creativity.

*Materials.* Paper; crayons; table or floor space.

*Before.* Lay out paper and crayons in a shared central space. Check that the area is easy for children and carers to access.

*During.* Invite children to select colours. Keep crayons in a shared central space. Encourage, never force, sharing.

*After.* Ask children whether they want to keep, display or take home their work. Pack materials away and record favourite colours, topics or conversations.

